<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Declaration of Sentiments</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1848">1848</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Declaration of Rights and Sentiments</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Declaration of Sentiments, or Declaration of Rights and Sentiments, is the document signed in 1848 by 68 women and 32 men at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-falls-convention">Seneca Falls Convention</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, the first women's rights convention organised by women. Its principal author, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-cady-stanton">Elizabeth Cady Stanton</a>, an organiser of the convention with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucretia-mott">Lucretia Mott</a>, modelled it on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-declaration-of-independence">United States Declaration of Independence</a>, declaring that all men and women are created equal. It listed women's grievances, among them the denial of the elective franchise, of property and of education, and demanded their immediate admission to all the rights of citizens. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-douglass">Frederick Douglass</a>'s support helped pass the convention's resolutions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/declaration-of-sentiments.jpg" alt="Declaration of Sentiments" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Library of Congress · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Declaration_sentiments_foote_lrg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the early 19th century American women were largely confined to domestic roles, and coverture laws subordinated wives to their husbands. Egalitarian ideas spread with the Second Great Awakening and its reform movements. Early advocates such as Frances Wright and Ernestine Rose concentrated on economic conditions and marriage laws, while abolitionism gave women a platform, and the anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> work of Angelina Grimke and her sister Sarah drew controversy. At the 1840 World Anti-Slavery Convention in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> the male delegates voted to bar the American women delegates from full participation, which moved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucretia-mott">Lucretia Mott</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-cady-stanton">Elizabeth Cady Stanton</a> to begin campaigning for women's rights.</p><p>The convention met over two days at the Wesleyan Methodist Church in Seneca Falls, organised by Stanton with Mott, Martha Coffin Wright and local Quaker women. About 300 people attended, around 40 of them men. Over two days they heard addresses by Stanton and Mott, voted on resolutions and debated the text of the Declaration, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-douglass">Frederick Douglass</a> argued for including suffrage in the convention's agenda.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The preamble follows <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a>'s text closely, changing “all men are created equal” to “all men and women are created equal”, and turning the patient sufferance of the colonies into that of “the women under this government”. By borrowing a familiar form, Stanton tied women's complaints to the founding document so that they would not seem overly radical.</p><p>The list of grievances that follows is addressed to man as Jefferson's was to the king: women had no voice in the laws they obeyed, a married woman was civilly dead in law, her wages belonged to her husband, divorce and guardianship laws ignored her, and the colleges and the ministry were closed to her. The closing pledged agents, tracts, petitions and further conventions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Only about 100 of the 300 present signed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-douglass">Frederick Douglass</a>'s newspaper, the North Star, called it the “grand movement for attaining the civil, social, political, and religious rights of women”, but the movement became mainstream only after the Civil War and Reconstruction. Among the signers, Catharine Paine registered to vote in Seattle in 1885, after Washington Territory gave women the vote in 1883; Rhoda Palmer, who voted in 1918 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> granted women suffrage, was the only woman signer ever to vote legally; and Charlotte Woodward was the only signer who lived to see the 19th amendment.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Declaration of Sentiments” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Declaration of Sentiments</b>, adopted at Seneca Falls in 1848 and modelled on the Declaration of Independence, declared “that all men and women are created equal”, listed the wrongs done to women by law and custom, and called on women to secure their “sacred right to the elective…</blockquote><a href="/movement/womens-suffrage-movement">Women's suffrage movement · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>After the War of 1812 it gained a wider following, and from the 1820s variations of it were issued to proclaim the rights of workers, farmers and women, among them the Seneca Falls Convention's <b>Declaration of Sentiments</b> of 1848.</blockquote><a href="/work/united-states-declaration-of-independence">United States Declaration of Independence · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its <b>Declaration of Sentiments</b>, stating that 'all men and women are created equal', launched the suffrage movement.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-19">19 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
